"""Multilingual ingredient names: French and Modern Standard Arabic (pure Arabic script, no
dialect, no Latin transliterations) for every canonical ingredient, plus the canonical entries
and English aliases added after the first dictionary shipped.

Data only. `backend/app/ingredients.py` merges this on top of its original English
dictionary, so nothing here can change what an already-known spelling resolves to.

Conventions
- The canonical key is always the English name (it is what the grocery list shows and what
  purchase marks are keyed on).
- Names are written naturally (accents, hamza, tashkeel-free Arabic with ة); the matcher
  folds accents, hamza seats, ى/ي, ة/ه and drops the definite article ال / le / la / l',
  so only list the spelling a person would write once.
- Lists are "|" separated. Give singular and plural where both are natural.
- Scope is exactly English + French + MSA. Do not add dialect words or Latin-letter Arabic.
"""


# English display name when it differs from the (ASCII) canonical key.
EN_DISPLAY: dict[str, str] = {"creme fraiche": "crème fraîche"}


def _split(value: str) -> list[str]:
    return [part.strip() for part in value.split("|") if part.strip()]


